// Experimental WASI mode demo: Fs.read via wasi:filesystem +
// wasi:io/streams (capa --wasm --component --wasi).
//
// Migrates Fs.read to the canonical WASI Preview 2 streaming read:
//
//   fs.read(path) -> descriptor.open-at (against the host-granted
//                    PREOPEN descriptor for the path's parent)
//                 -> descriptor.read-via-stream(offset = 0)
//                 -> input-stream.blocking-read LOOP, accumulating the
//                    chunks until stream-error::closed (EOF, the normal
//                    terminator; NOT an error) -> Ok(String)
//                 -> all OWN resource handles (descriptor, input-stream,
//                    and any error a failed read carries) are dropped on
//                    every exit path; the preopen ROOT descriptors are
//                    never dropped.
//
// This is the FIRST touch-point that uses wasi:io/streams. The Ok(String)
// is byte-identical to the Python backend's f.read() (UTF-8) and to the
// capa:host bridge; an unreadable / missing file is an Err(IoError).
// Stdio stays on capa:host -- the hybrid coexistence this mode validates.
// See docs/design/wasi_mode.md.
//
// The PREOPEN CEILING applies exactly as for the metadata ops: every
// Fs path here is a string LITERAL, the compiler derives the parent
// directories to preopen (READ_ONLY for read), and a path outside every
// preopen is unreachable. A DYNAMIC read path is rejected at compile
// time in --wasi mode (fail-closed). The same program runs unchanged on
// the default capa:host backend. Fs.write / Fs.list_dir and the fine
// attenuators Fs.restrict_to / Fs.allows are still rejected under --wasi
// (later increments).
//
// The file below lives under a `data` directory next to this file:
//
//   mkdir -p examples/wasm/data
//   printf 'hello from a capability-secured read\n' > examples/wasm/data/file.txt
//   capa --wasm --component --wasi --run examples/wasm/wasi_fs_read.capa
//
// (Adjust the literal prefix below to an absolute path you control if
// your runner's working directory differs.)

fun main(fs: Fs, stdio: Stdio)
    let r = fs.read("examples/wasm/data/file.txt")
    match r
        Ok(contents) -> stdio.println("read ok, contents:\n${contents}")
        Err(e) -> stdio.println("read failed: ${e}")
    // A path inside the preopen that does not exist is a clean Err.
    let missing = fs.read("examples/wasm/data/missing.txt")
    match missing
        Ok(contents) -> stdio.println("unexpected ok: ${contents}")
        Err(e) -> stdio.println("missing read failed (expected): err")
